{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-65508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-65508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for cosine gives  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =2 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-65508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a30ee3bd372f4c1bcd64bf34773da5d03d311eaa04d1a7b4a25df653423902f"} {"question" "q-65508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-65508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-65508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =60\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a30ee3bd372f4c1bcd64bf34773da5d03d311eaa04d1a7b4a25df653423902f"} {"question" "q-327713" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327713" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a30ee3bd372f4c1bcd64bf34773da5d03d311eaa04d1a7b4a25df653423902f"} {"question" "q-327714" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327714" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "30"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=$\n {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(3\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+27 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =36 \\\\ R & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+{60}^{∘})\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a30ee3bd372f4c1bcd64bf34773da5d03d311eaa04d1a7b4a25df653423902f"} {"question" "q-327990" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327990" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327990" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884/state.json" "file_sha256" "a25d93fa564a86871f32b175b683d770e008432062282683c0e00596cb67592e"} {"question" "q-327990" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60"] "cate